    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Mali Empire was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a> state founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita">Sundiata Keita</a> that dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> from the 13th century.</li><li>Its gold from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambuk">Bambuk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bure">Bure</a> fed trans-Saharan trade and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a> centres of commerce and Islamic learning.</li><li>Succession disputes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> attacks and shifting trade routes shrank it to its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manding">Manding</a> heartland by the 1600s.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a> spent so much gold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> on his 1324 pilgrimage that it was remembered for years, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> soon appeared on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catalan-atlas">Catalan Atlas</a> of 1375.</p></aside>
    </section>

